1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,540
It's almost two in the morning, on
the twenty-first of August, 1968.

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:08,820
In Prague, a radio announcer
begins to read a statement.

3
00:00:09,527 --> 00:00:12,167
Foreign armies have crossed
the border during the night.

4
00:00:12,247 --> 00:00:14,727
And the statement says it happened without

5
00:00:14,957 --> 00:00:17,207
the knowledge of the
country's own leaders.

6
00:00:17,881 --> 00:00:21,501
As he reads, the country's
medium-wave transmitters go silent.

7
00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:23,901
Someone has switched them off.

8
00:00:24,486 --> 00:00:27,046
Across the country, radios hiss.

9
00:00:27,586 --> 00:00:32,226
But in kitchens, offices and workshops,
a small box on the wall keeps talking.

10
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,680
It carries the statement
to the very last word.

11
00:00:36,928 --> 00:00:40,448
That box had no tuning dial. No antenna.

12
00:00:40,688 --> 00:00:42,628
No buttons for other stations.

13
00:00:43,491 --> 00:00:46,451
One cable, one volume
knob, and one programme...

14
00:00:46,531 --> 00:00:47,991
whatever came through the wall.

15
00:00:48,835 --> 00:00:51,215
So how did the one radio
that couldn't choose anything

16
00:00:51,445 --> 00:00:54,335
end up carrying the news
everyone else had lost?

17
00:00:55,369 --> 00:00:57,529
Because it wasn't really a radio.

18
00:00:57,649 --> 00:01:00,689
It never picked up a single radio wave.

19
00:01:01,397 --> 00:01:04,737
In Czech its name simply
meant broadcasting by wire.

20
00:01:05,557 --> 00:01:08,817
Most people shortened that
to a nickname: the wire box.

21
00:01:09,602 --> 00:01:14,702
Not the loudspeakers on poles in the
village square. This one lived indoors.

22
00:01:15,503 --> 00:01:19,763
The programme travelled from a central
station, through cables, into the home.

23
00:01:20,503 --> 00:01:23,003
Like a telephone call that never ended.

24
00:01:23,579 --> 00:01:26,055
At the amplifier, the signal
was stepped up to a hundred and

25
00:01:26,285 --> 00:01:30,459
twenty volts, so it could run
for kilometres along the line.

26
00:01:31,035 --> 00:01:34,975
Near the listener, a transformer
brought it back down to thirty volts.

27
00:01:35,397 --> 00:01:37,877
And inside the box,
there was almost nothing.

28
00:01:38,637 --> 00:01:43,097
A transformer, a loudspeaker, and a
knob that clicked between volume steps.

29
00:01:43,836 --> 00:01:47,276
Nothing to tune... because
there was nothing to tune into.

30
00:01:47,664 --> 00:01:50,764
And switching off a transmitter
does nothing to a cable.

31
00:01:51,484 --> 00:01:55,104
That night in 1968, the
wire simply kept going.

32
00:01:56,077 --> 00:01:59,657
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

33
00:01:59,957 --> 00:02:01,957
It really helps the channel.

34
00:02:02,796 --> 00:02:04,696
But that only makes it stranger.

35
00:02:05,656 --> 00:02:07,436
Why would a country build
a whole second radio

36
00:02:07,666 --> 00:02:10,996
network, cable by cable,
into people's homes?

37
00:02:12,019 --> 00:02:15,439
The first Czechoslovak
lines went live in 1953.

38
00:02:15,519 --> 00:02:18,979
And the idea came from the Soviet Union.

39
00:02:19,605 --> 00:02:24,185
The Soviets began building wired
radio networks in the 1920s.

40
00:02:24,905 --> 00:02:29,285
A simple loudspeaker was far cheaper
than a conventional radio receiver.

41
00:02:29,985 --> 00:02:34,025
For millions of families, it was
their only way to listen to the radio.

42
00:02:34,745 --> 00:02:36,605
They called it the radio point.

43
00:02:37,445 --> 00:02:40,685
In its simplest form, one socket
in the wall, one programme

44
00:02:40,765 --> 00:02:43,905
from the central service,
or from a local station.

45
00:02:44,678 --> 00:02:47,638
For a government, it solved
two problems at once.

46
00:02:48,318 --> 00:02:50,798
It was cheap to put a
voice into every home...

47
00:02:50,918 --> 00:02:52,778
and it was the government's voice.

48
00:02:53,137 --> 00:02:54,917
And the reasoning was military.

49
00:02:55,677 --> 00:02:59,417
In a war, a radio transmitter
is a big, obvious target.

50
00:03:00,147 --> 00:03:03,187
A major transmitter could
serve an entire region.

51
00:03:03,927 --> 00:03:07,227
If it went down, a huge
area could lose its signal.

52
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:10,800
A cable network doesn't
depend on one big mast.

53
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:15,590
It has its own amplifiers,
its own lines, its own route.

54
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:19,020
Knock out the transmitter,
and the wire can still talk.

55
00:03:19,652 --> 00:03:25,612
It wasn't invincible. The amplifiers
needed electricity. Cables could be cut.

56
00:03:25,692 --> 00:03:27,592
It was a spare route, not a fortress.

57
00:03:28,177 --> 00:03:30,577
And it had everyday advantages, too.

58
00:03:31,177 --> 00:03:33,417
No need to fight a weak indoor signal.

59
00:03:33,497 --> 00:03:36,857
No radio interference to contend with.

60
00:03:37,217 --> 00:03:40,397
And a receiver so simple
that it cost very little.

61
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:43,810
But the design had one more consequence.

62
00:03:43,970 --> 00:03:48,070
And a one-party state could hardly
have asked for a better one.

63
00:03:48,783 --> 00:03:50,143
The listener couldn't choose.

64
00:03:50,223 --> 00:03:52,303
The programme was decided for you.

65
00:03:52,663 --> 00:03:54,223
One cable, one voice.

66
00:03:54,918 --> 00:03:56,598
Not all of it was propaganda.

67
00:03:57,438 --> 00:04:01,218
There was music, stories for
children, radio plays, the weather.

68
00:04:01,918 --> 00:04:04,998
Plenty of people left it on
all day, just for company.

69
00:04:05,363 --> 00:04:09,083
But all of it arrived through a channel
the state owned from end to end.

70
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:13,823
There was no dial to turn to
Vienna, or to Radio Free Europe.

71
00:04:14,485 --> 00:04:17,105
What the state couldn't
control was the off switch.

72
00:04:17,185 --> 00:04:20,205
Or whether anyone was actually listening.

73
00:04:20,598 --> 00:04:23,598
And many homes had an
ordinary radio as well.

74
00:04:24,298 --> 00:04:27,558
The box for the kitchen, the
real radio for the evening...

75
00:04:27,798 --> 00:04:30,198
and, discreetly, for
stations from the West.

76
00:04:30,715 --> 00:04:34,675
So it's tempting to call it a
control device dressed up as a radio.

77
00:04:35,376 --> 00:04:38,516
Except that democracies
wired up radio too.

78
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:42,300
Switzerland started its
own version in 1931.

79
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:46,280
In deep mountain valleys,
reception was often poor,

80
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:49,320
so programmes went down
the telephone line instead.

81
00:04:50,026 --> 00:04:53,426
Italy launched a similar
service in 1958, also

82
00:04:53,656 --> 00:04:56,426
over telephone lines,
with several channels...

83
00:04:56,826 --> 00:04:59,506
including one of nothing
but classical music.

84
00:04:59,849 --> 00:05:02,809
And in British towns, from the late 1920s,

85
00:05:02,939 --> 00:05:06,409
companies rented out
loudspeakers connected by wire.

86
00:05:07,091 --> 00:05:09,751
The same idea, doing very different jobs.

87
00:05:10,315 --> 00:05:12,055
In the Alps, it beat the mountains.

88
00:05:12,975 --> 00:05:14,655
In Italy, it offered choice.

89
00:05:15,475 --> 00:05:17,655
In Czechoslovakia, it was
a spare route for war...

90
00:05:17,885 --> 00:05:20,475
with one channel, chosen by the state.

91
00:05:21,125 --> 00:05:23,905
The technology didn't
decide what it was for.

92
00:05:24,345 --> 00:05:27,505
That depended on who controlled
the network... and the programme.

93
00:05:28,719 --> 00:05:31,859
And in August 1968, the
people controlling the

94
00:05:32,089 --> 00:05:34,979
transmitters turned out
not to control every route.

95
00:05:37,659 --> 00:05:42,499
Back to that night. Armies of five
Warsaw Pact countries, led by the Soviet

96
00:05:42,729 --> 00:05:45,999
Union, had crossed the border
to end the Prague Spring...

97
00:05:46,299 --> 00:05:50,419
months in which the Communist Party
itself had tried to loosen its grip.

98
00:05:51,005 --> 00:05:53,405
The announcer, Vladimír Fišer, was reading

99
00:05:53,635 --> 00:05:55,925
a statement from the
party's own leadership.

100
00:05:56,925 --> 00:06:00,425
The troops, it said, had come without
the knowledge of the president,

101
00:06:00,505 --> 00:06:03,825
parliament, the government, or
the party's first secretary.

102
00:06:04,243 --> 00:06:08,803
And it called the invasion a denial of
the basic norms of international law.

103
00:06:09,173 --> 00:06:11,253
But the man in charge of the
country's communications,

104
00:06:11,483 --> 00:06:15,653
Karel Hoffmann, was a hardliner
who backed the invasion.

105
00:06:16,513 --> 00:06:21,113
He had the medium-wave transmitters
shut down just as those words went out.

106
00:06:21,687 --> 00:06:26,267
Here's the irony. A network built
in case foreign armies came.

107
00:06:26,807 --> 00:06:29,587
And on the night they came,
the transmitters went dark...

108
00:06:29,687 --> 00:06:31,807
but the wired network stayed on.

109
00:06:32,147 --> 00:06:36,467
So the statement reached
listeners in full. Only by wire.

110
00:06:37,195 --> 00:06:39,455
The wire didn't stop a single tank.

111
00:06:40,055 --> 00:06:42,835
It was cables and
amplifiers, nothing more.

112
00:06:43,215 --> 00:06:46,075
But for those first hours,
the box that couldn't choose

113
00:06:46,305 --> 00:06:49,475
anything at all was the one that
told people what was happening.

114
00:06:51,685 --> 00:06:53,605
And it wasn't the end of the story.

115
00:06:54,205 --> 00:06:57,225
Later that morning, the
broadcast fell silent again.

116
00:06:57,825 --> 00:06:59,685
Around eleven, it came back...

117
00:06:59,765 --> 00:07:03,505
first from a bunker-like emergency
studio elsewhere in Prague.

118
00:07:04,223 --> 00:07:06,523
Then from a studio the
troops had missed...

119
00:07:06,803 --> 00:07:10,763
hidden inside the radio building, with
soldiers already in its corridors.

120
00:07:11,657 --> 00:07:14,477
In the days that followed,
studios across the country

121
00:07:14,707 --> 00:07:17,937
took turns keeping the
broadcast going, like a relay.

122
00:07:18,644 --> 00:07:20,464
The wire was one backup.

123
00:07:21,044 --> 00:07:23,884
The people running the radio
turned out to be another.

124
00:07:26,304 --> 00:07:30,284
And Karel Hoffmann? He
didn't fall. He rose.

125
00:07:31,044 --> 00:07:34,544
For the next two decades, he sat
near the top of the Communist Party.

126
00:07:35,281 --> 00:07:37,141
The regime ended in 1989.

127
00:07:37,371 --> 00:07:43,681
And in 2003, thirty-five years after
that night, a Czech appeals court

128
00:07:43,911 --> 00:07:48,201
convicted him of sabotage, for the
order to switch off the transmitters.

129
00:07:48,691 --> 00:07:52,011
He went to prison in 2004,
at the age of eighty...

130
00:07:52,091 --> 00:07:55,371
and was released less than a
month later, on health grounds.

131
00:07:58,091 --> 00:08:01,091
The wire outlived his
career, but not by much.

132
00:08:01,771 --> 00:08:03,031
Cheap radios got better.

133
00:08:03,261 --> 00:08:09,211
FM brought clearer sound and several
stations, with no cable to every building.

134
00:08:09,826 --> 00:08:13,926
Next to that, a radio with one
programme had nothing left to offer.

135
00:08:14,530 --> 00:08:17,890
After 1989, the networks
kept running for a while.

136
00:08:18,590 --> 00:08:20,890
Then, one by one, they were switched off.

137
00:08:21,630 --> 00:08:24,430
In 1999, the last one went silent.

138
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:30,520
It's easy to remember the
box as a propaganda speaker.

139
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:34,820
And it was built inside a system
that decided what people heard.

140
00:08:35,209 --> 00:08:37,469
But the same idea carried
radio through the Swiss

141
00:08:37,699 --> 00:08:40,829
mountains, and classical
music across Italy.

142
00:08:41,149 --> 00:08:44,249
And for one night in
1968, the wire carried

143
00:08:44,479 --> 00:08:46,769
the very news someone
had tried to silence.

144
00:08:47,581 --> 00:08:49,781
A wire doesn't care what it carries.

145
00:08:50,441 --> 00:08:52,781
What matters is who holds the other end...

146
00:08:53,217 --> 00:08:55,527
and whether there's
another radio in the house.
